[
    {
        "content": "<p>If I list examples of what I've seen, then we have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monomorphisms, Epimorphisms</li>\n<li>Limits, Colimits</li>\n<li>Weighted Limits, Weighted Colimits</li>\n<li>Weak Kan Extensions, Pointwise Kan Extensions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I'm not as familiar with Kan Lifts, but I assume there is a notion of preservation for these too.</p>\n<p>I've been trying to figure out if these are instances of a common concept:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The property of being a monomorphism can be phrased as a particular diagram being a pullback, so one can fold preservation of monos/epis into preservation of a particular limit/colimit.</li>\n<li><em>Pointwise</em> kan extensions can be viewed as a type of weighted (co)limit, but weak kan extensions generally aren't. Indeed, one of the equivalent definitions of pointwise kan extensions are those kan extensions \"preserved by all representables\" (this has to be interpreted carefully for left vs right extensions). Absoluteness of kan extensions also comes to mind.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are also structures like \"commutative diagrams\" and \"isomorphisms\" which all functors preserve - I suppose what I'm focusing on are structures that may not be preserved by a functor.</p>\n<p>Initially I thought the answer might be \"universal element\", since that seems to be a common thread behind these structures, all being instances of universal properties. But this is a bit too general!</p>\n<p>Namely, if we have some presheaf <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span></span></span></span> on a category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span></span></span></span> for which we have a universal element <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u \\in P(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>, and some functor <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mspace></mspace><mspace width=\"0.1111em\"/><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0.17em\"></mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">:</mo><mspace width=\"0.3333em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F \\colon \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1111em;\"></span><span class=\"mpunct\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\">:</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.3333em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span>, it's not entirely clear how one would \"apply\" <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span></span></span></span>. Of course, there are ways to transport <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span></span></span></span> along <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> to define a presheaf on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span> - the natural choice is via left kan extension, using cocompleteness of the presheaf category. But this sends representable functors to representable functors and is functorial, which would mean that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> automatically preserves <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span></span></span></span> (meaning, sends it to another universal element). We could instead try the right kan extension, but then the result of applying <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span></span></span></span> can't easily be interpreted as a candidate universal element for some presheaf on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>So presheaves are a bit too general - there's something special about the universal properties associated to (weighted) limits/colimits and kan extensions that allows one to make sense of their preservation, and this doesn't seem to make sense for arbitrary universal properties. But I've not been able to distill what precisely that special quality is...</p>\n<p>I'd also be interested in analogs of the question above for reflection, lifting and creation of structure.</p>",
        "id": 642995028,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruby Khondaker (she/her)",
        "timestamp": 1795456624
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>After writing this I realised that power objects can also potentially be preserved by functors, and those don't seem to fit into weighted limits or kan extensions.</p>",
        "id": 642996284,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruby Khondaker (she/her)",
        "timestamp": 1795456836
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Power objects have a universal property, though, and I'm fairly sure I once worked out how they can be seen as arising from an adjunction. This must be known, but I can't look for it right now.</p>",
        "id": 643128940,
        "sender_full_name": "David Michael Roberts",
        "timestamp": 1795490195
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right, but as far as I can tell “universal property” is too general to admit a notion of preservation by a functor.</p>",
        "id": 643208361,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruby Khondaker (she/her)",
        "timestamp": 1795516443
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In terms of the adjunction associated to a power object - morphisms <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>Y</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to \\Omega^Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Ω</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> naturally correspond to subobjects of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\times Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span>, which should naturally correspond to morphisms <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y \\to \\Omega^X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Ω</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. So it's mutually adjoint on the right with itself, I suppose. Not sure if this is what you meant?</p>",
        "id": 643210514,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruby Khondaker (she/her)",
        "timestamp": 1795516938
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sure, there's an adjunction, and of course <a href=\"https://preview.1lab.dev/390/Cat.Functor.Kan.Adjoint.html\">https://preview.1lab.dev/390/Cat.Functor.Kan.Adjoint.html</a></p>",
        "id": 643229899,
        "sender_full_name": "David Michael Roberts",
        "timestamp": 1795521523
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This still doesn't make \"preserving power objects\" a special case of \"preserving Kan extensions\" though -- as can be seen by the fact that the Kan extension presenting an adjoint is <em>absolute</em> and thus preserved by <em>any</em> functor.</p>",
        "id": 643232679,
        "sender_full_name": "James Deikun",
        "timestamp": 1795522118
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In general though I think a functor can \"preserve\", and perhaps \"lift\" and \"create\", any kind of stuff/structure/property that is 2-monadic (or pseudomonadic?) over Cat.</p>",
        "id": 643233906,
        "sender_full_name": "James Deikun",
        "timestamp": 1795522418
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh! I don’t know much about the theory of 2-monads, but could you perhaps outline how to extract a notion of preservation from such a 2-monad?</p>",
        "id": 643234944,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruby Khondaker (she/her)",
        "timestamp": 1795522637
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's basically a weakening of the usual way homomorphisms preserve stuff in algebra--the preserving functor commutes with the structure functors up to unique isomorphism.  So for example, for all appropriate diagrams <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span>, the \"designated\" limit cone of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FD</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span> is uniquely isomorphic to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> composed with the \"designated\" limit cone of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span>.</p>",
        "id": 643237504,
        "sender_full_name": "James Deikun",
        "timestamp": 1795523217
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(There's also a stricter \"on the nose\" notion of preservation, but this one is usually the one you want, especially for something specified by universal properties so it is unique up to unique iso if it exists.)</p>",
        "id": 643239934,
        "sender_full_name": "James Deikun",
        "timestamp": 1795523774
    }
]